person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shioyama</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Matsueda</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Horihata</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Post-TIPS hepatic encephalopathy treated by occlusion balloon-assisted retrograde embolization of a coexisting spontaneous splenorenal shunt</article-title><source>Cardiovasc Intervent Radiol</source><year>1996</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>53</fpage><lpage>55</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8653749</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B31"><label>31</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>LaBerge</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Somberg</surname><given-names>KA</given-names></name><name><surname>Lake</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Two-year outcome following transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt for variceal bleeding: results in 90 patients</article-title><source>Gastroenterology</source><year>1995</year><volume>108</volume><fpage>1143</fpage><lpage>1151</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7698582</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B32"><label>32</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jorge</surname><given-names>EC</given-names></name><name><surname>Chantal</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Prema</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Augmentation of portal blood flow improves function of human cirrhotic liver</article-title><source>Hepatology</source><year>1994</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>375</fpage><lpage>380</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8294094</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B33"><label>33</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pomier-Layrargues</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>TIPS and hepatic encephalopathy</article-title><source>Semin Liver DIS</source><year>1996</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>315</fpage><lpage>320</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8989816</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B34"><label>34</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Conner</surname><given-names>WE</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoak</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Warner</surname><given-names>ED</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Massive thrombosis produced by fatty acid infusion</article-title><source>J Clin Invest</source><year>1963</year><volume>42</volume><fpage>860</fpage><lpage>866</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14022629</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B35"><label>35</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hoak</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Warner</surname><given-names>ED</given-names></name><name><surname>Conner</surname><given-names>WE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Platelets, fatty acids and thrombosis</article-title><source>Circ Res</source><year>1967</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>11</fpage><lpage>17</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4959657</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Balloon-occluded retrograde transvenous obliteration of gastric varix.</p><p><bold>A.</bold> CT scan obtained before balloon-occluded retrograde transvenous obliteration shows dilated gastric varix (arrowheads) and gastrorenal shunt (arrow).</p><p><bold>B.</bold> Narrow segment of the shunt tract (arrow) is demonstrated at angiography.</p><p><bold>C.</bold> Gastric varix (small arrows), gastrorenal shunt (large arrow), and its multiple fine collaterals (arrowheads) are seen at angiography. In the narrow segment, an inflated balloon catheter (long arrow) is visible.</p><p><bold>D.</bold> With the balloon inflated (arrow), 2&#x000d7;2&#x000d7;2-mm gelatin sponge particles were injected into the gastrorenal shunt, from where they flowed into collateral veins. Subsequent angiography shows that the fine collaterals have disappeared.</p><p><bold>E.</bold> Three hours after the injection of ethanolamine oleate into the varix, as much as possible of the remaining sclerosing agent was aspirated. Fluoroscopy shows that some, however, was retained.</p><p><bold>F.</bold> Immediate follow-up angiography demonstrates obliteration of both the varix and gastrorenal shunt.</p><p><bold>G.</bold> Follow-up CT scan obtained three months after balloon-occluded retrograde transvenous obliteration depicts contraction of the varix (arrowheads) and gastrorenal shunt (arrow), with deposition of sclerosing agent.</p><p><bold>H.</bold> One year later, there is no trace of either the varix or shunt.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjr-4-109-g001"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Summary of 21 Patients with Gastric Variceal Bleeding Treated by TIPS and/or Balloon-Occluded Retrograde Transvenous Obliteration</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjr-4-109-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>Note.-Pre = pre-TIPS or pre-BRTO, Post = post-TIPS or post-BRTO, TIPS = transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt, BRTO = balloon-occluded retrograde transvenous obliteration of gastric varix</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>